# What this corpus draws no page for. One entry per line -- a basename
# for a program that produces no page at all, a basename and " pN" for
# one page of a program that produces others -- each with the reason it
# draws nothing.
#
# A run compares the pages both engines drew, so anything that draws
# none is outside the comparison and the run answers nothing about it.
# Saying so here in advance is what keeps that from passing for a
# result: the count of programs a corpus evaluated is honest and says
# only that they were reached, so a run in which every program failed
# reports exactly what a run in which every one rendered reports. An
# absence nobody declared fails the corpus for that reason. So does a
# declared absence that turns out to have rendered -- the entry's reason
# has lapsed, and the line is left telling its next reader something
# untrue.
#
# The twelve below are listings that ask for no page. A
# book teaches by the smallest program that shows the thing, and for a
# prologue, a query, a font written to disk or an error handler that is
# the program with no page in it: the chapter's script would supply the
# page, and the chapter's script is prose. They are still run, because
# what they exercise is reached whether or not it ends in ink.
#
# bb_05-centered-dash-patterns -- a fault in the listing as printed.
#           It computes the offset of a centred dash pattern with
#           "pathlen last sub patternlength idiv", and the pathlength
#           it wrote for itself sums square roots along a flattened
#           path and so answers with a real. idiv takes two integers
#           and a real is a typecheck (PLRM 8.2, idiv), which the first
#           of the five centerdash calls raises with 276.0 and 60 on
#           the stack. Nothing is stroked and the showpage at the foot
#           is never reached. div, and a cvi where an integer is meant,
#           is what the line wanted; the listing is kept as printed.
#
# gb_02-01-listing -- three orderings of the operands of findfont,
#           scalefont and setfont, to show which of them leave the
#           stack as the next line expects. It sets three fonts and
#           marks nothing.
#
# gb_02-05-listing -- the skeleton of a print server's main loop, with
#           a save and restore around each job. Its inner test reads
#           "any port active", which is the book's prose standing where
#           a condition would go rather than operators the language
#           has, and the loop it sits in was built after the save the
#           line above restores to. It reaches neither the job it would
#           run nor any page of its own.
#
# gb_09-05-listing -- the prologue of a colour separation. It defines a
#           setcmykcolor that keeps one component and turns it into a
#           grey, sets that component's screen angle, and leaves a
#           comment where the file being separated would be placed.
#
# gb_10-03-listing -- a query job, which is a job that asks rather than
#           prints: whether a font is in FontDirectory, and how much VM
#           is free. Both answers go to the standard output.
#
# gb_12-01-listing -- document structuring comments for font
#           inclusion. Everything that is not a comment is a save and a
#           restore, twice.
#
# gb_12-02-listing -- an unencapsulated job. It asks to leave job
#           encapsulation with "serverdict begin 0 exitserver" so that
#           the timestamp it defines afterwards survives into the next
#           job, and a job that alters initial VM is not a job that
#           draws.
#
# gb_13-02-listing -- writes rather than draws. It opens
#           fonts/Logo-Font for output and copies the Type 3 font
#           program in its own tail into it, so that a later job can
#           read the font back; the listing names showpage nowhere.
#
# gb_14-02-listing -- its showpage is data, not code. The listing runs
#           an included job through stopped, and the job calls
#           triggererror, which is undefined on purpose so that the
#           handler has something to catch. The handler prints the
#           error and then reads with readline as far as a
#           %ENDuserexec line, which is what discards the rest of the
#           failed job -- and the included job's showpage is one of the
#           lines it reads. What follows the handler prints the name of
#           the job that continues and stops.
#
# gb_14-03-listing -- wraps errordict's limitcheck so that a job which
#           runs out of VM reports how much was left before the
#           standard handler takes over. A prologue for other jobs.
#
# gb_14-04-listing -- wraps handleerror so that the standard report is
#           followed by the operand stack the error left. A prologue
#           for other jobs.
#
# gb_15-02-listing -- prints one line from the prologue and one from
#           the trailer, so that a job which dies in between says how
#           far it got. There is nothing else in it.
